        "__dashboardUid__": "av_rooms_dashboard",
        "__panelId__": "900",
        "description": "- Targeted-count drop SLO (critical tier only)\n  The absolute targeted device count fell below 90% of its trailing-day average while coverage (a ratio) can still read ~1.0 - i.e. EAM/LanDB returned a shrunken device set and a chunk of the fleet is silently unmonitored.\n  Scoped to `tier=~\"critical|\"` and deliberately NOT grouped by tier. All four tiers sweep the same fleet, so one continuously-running tier is sufficient to observe the fleet size, and the critical tier is the only one whose series never goes stale between evaluations (`3.7s x devices/threads` sizing; `*/5` cadence). An untiered `sum()` is actively broken after the split: Prometheus staleness keeps each tier's series alive for ~5 min after it publishes, so the sum oscillates between ~1x and ~4x the fleet while the 1d average settles in between, and the `< 0.9 x avg` test fires permanently. The empty alternation branch keeps the expression correct before the split (no `tier` label exists yet) and keeps the 1d baseline continuous across it, because `sum()` yields the same unlabelled series on both sides.\n  `< bool` (not a bare `<`) is required: a bare filtering comparison returns an EMPTY result when the fleet is healthy, which Grafana reports as NoData, and this rule's `noDataState: Alerting` would turn every healthy evaluation into a page. With `bool` the expression is always 0 or 1 while the metric exists, so NoData once again means only what it should mean here - the metric stopped being published at all - and `noDataState: Alerting` stays correct (see the noDataState trap in ALERTS_NOTES.md).\n  Action: check the last run-eam / run-landb sync and the EAM/LanDB source data.",
