        "__dashboardUid__": "av_rooms_dashboard",
        "__panelId__": "900",
        "description": "- Fleet-empty guardrail (SLO)\n  avtools_snmp_devices_targeted has read 0 (or been entirely absent) for a sustained 10m while the snmp-timeseries job is still nominally running \u2014 the device fleet used for collection is empty. This is the explicit backstop for the 2026-07-30 outage class: an upstream LanDB/EAM fetch failure emptied the fleet table, run-landb still exited status=ok, and snmp-timeseries short-circuited on skipped_no_targets *before* publishing any metric, so the series vanished instead of reading zero. Coverage-ratio SLOs read as 0/0 (no data) in that case; this rule fires on either outcome \u2014 explicit zero once the app emits it on the skipped path, or absence before/without that fix.\n  Scoped to `tier=~\"critical|\"` after the tier split: summing every tier would let another tier's still-stale non-zero sample (Prometheus keeps a series current for ~5 min after its last publish) hold the sum above zero for minutes after the fleet actually emptied, and would delay or suppress this guardrail. The critical tier publishes every 5 min, so its value is always current, and it sweeps the same fleet as the other three. The empty alternation branch matches today's untiered series, so the rule is unchanged in behaviour until the tiered CronJobs deploy; a bare `{tier=\"critical\"}` would match nothing today and, with `noDataState: Alerting`, page instantly.\n  Action: check run-eam / run-landb for a swallowed upstream fetch failure (its own freshness alert may not have caught it if the run still logged status=ok) and check the snmp-timeseries CronJob logs for skipped_no_targets.",
